    'body' => <<<'HTML'
<div class="key-takeaways"><h2>Key takeaways</h2><ul>
<li>A <strong>roof damage public adjuster</strong> is a licensed adjuster who represents you, the policyholder, on a roof claim. The adjuster your insurer sends represents the insurer.</li>
<li>NOAA records 180 hail reports and 109 thunderstorm wind reports in Hays County, with hail peaking in April and May, so roof damage is a recurring risk for San Marcos homeowners.</li>
<li>Most roof disputes come down to five issues: storm damage vs. wear and tear, repair vs. replacement, matching, code-required items, and how your policy values the roof (replacement cost, actual cash value, or a roof payment schedule).</li>
<li>Good evidence is specific: dated photos of every slope, collateral damage, storm records for your address, and a line-item estimate.</li>
<li>Public adjuster fees in Texas are capped at 10% of the settlement and must be in a written contract you can cancel within 72 hours.</li>
</ul></div>

<p>After a spring storm rolls across San Marcos, most homeowners do the same thing: walk out to the yard, look up, and try to decide whether the roof is “bad enough” for a claim. That question is harder than it looks. Hail bruises can be invisible from the ground, a wind-lifted shingle can reseal and hide a broken bond, and an insurer’s estimate can treat a storm loss as age-related wear. This guide explains how roof claims are actually decided, where they tend to go sideways, and when a <strong>roof damage public adjuster</strong> may be worth bringing in.</p>

<p>It is general information from the perspective of a licensed Texas public adjuster, not legal advice. Every claim depends on your policy language and the facts of your loss.</p>

<figure><img src="/wp-content/uploads/2026/02/Roof-Damage-Public-Adjuster-1024x683.webp" alt="Adjuster in a hard hat kneeling on a shingle roof and inspecting storm damage" width="1024" height="683" loading="lazy" decoding="async"><figcaption>Roof claims are won or lost on documentation: every slope, every impact, and every damaged component.</figcaption></figure>

<h2>Why roof claims are so common in San Marcos and Hays County</h2>

<p>San Marcos sits on I-35 along the Balcones Escarpment, about 25 miles south of Austin, and spring thunderstorms are a regular part of life here. The National Weather Service office that covers the city is NWS Austin/San Antonio in New Braunfels. NOAA’s Storm Events Database shows how often severe weather reaches Hays County:</p>

<table>
<thead><tr><th>Hays County storm record (NOAA NCEI, 1953–2026)</th><th>Count</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Hail reports (1967–2026)</td><td>180</td></tr>
<tr><td>Hail 1.75" (golf ball) or larger</td><td>58</td></tr>
<tr><td>Hail 2.75" (baseball) or larger</td><td>8</td></tr>
<tr><td>Peak hail months</td><td>May (67 reports), April (57), March (23)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Thunderstorm wind reports</td><td>109 (peak in May and June)</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>

<p>Several of those events hit roofs directly. On <a href="https://www.ncdc.noaa.gov/stormevents/eventdetails.jsp?id=5135628" target="_blank" rel="noopener">March 16, 2000</a>, 4.5-inch hail just north of San Marcos caused widespread roof and window damage. On <a href="https://www.ncdc.noaa.gov/stormevents/eventdetails.jsp?id=1181388" target="_blank" rel="noopener">May 9, 2024</a>, 3-inch hail fell at San Marcos Academy west of the city and 2.5-inch hail was reported near the outlet mall, while 70 mph gusts damaged roofs and brought trees down in town. On <a href="https://www.ncdc.noaa.gov/stormevents/eventdetails.jsp?id=835457" target="_blank" rel="noopener">June 9, 2019</a>, an estimated 75 mph gust partially peeled the metal roof off a house in San Marcos. You can look up storms near your own address with our <a href="/storm-lookup/">storm lookup tool</a>, and our <a href="/san-marcos-hail-season-storm-data/">San Marcos hail season data</a> breaks the record down further.</p>

<p>The local housing stock matters too. Composition shingle roofs dominate the newer subdivisions along the I-35 growth corridor. Hill Country homes, barns, and outbuildings west of town often have standing-seam or exposed-fastener metal roofs. Older homes near downtown may have multiple roof layers, and commercial buildings along the interstate frequently have low-slope roofs. Each roof type shows storm damage differently, and each raises different claim questions.</p>

<h2>What insurers look for when they evaluate a roof claim</h2>

<p>The adjuster your insurer assigns, whether a staff adjuster or an independent adjuster hired for the storm, is usually trying to answer four questions:</p>

<ol>
<li><strong>Is there physical damage?</strong> Fractured or bruised shingles, punctures, displaced or missing shingles, creased tabs, dented metal panels, damaged flashing, vents, or ridge caps.</li>
<li><strong>Was it caused by a covered event?</strong> Most homeowners policies cover sudden wind and hail damage but exclude wear and tear, deterioration, and defects in workmanship.</li>
<li><strong>When did it happen?</strong> The insurer may compare the damage to its own weather data and ask whether the date of loss you reported lines up with a real storm.</li>
<li><strong>How much will it cost to fix, and how does the policy value it?</strong> That includes repair vs. replacement, deductibles, depreciation, and any roof-specific endorsements.</li>
</ol>

<p>None of that is unreasonable. The problem is that the answers often depend on judgment calls, such as how many impacts per test square justify replacing a slope, or whether granule loss is from hail or from age. Those are the judgment calls a public adjuster is trained to examine and, where the evidence supports it, push back on.</p>

<h2>Five roof claim disputes we see most often</h2>

<h3>1. Storm damage vs. wear and tear</h3>
<p>This is the most common roof dispute in Texas. An insurer may attribute cracking, granule loss, or lifted tabs to age, heat, or poor installation rather than to a storm. The evidence that tends to matter is specific: fresh impact marks with exposed mat and sharp edges, a random impact pattern across slopes, matching dents on soft metals (vents, gutters, downspouts, AC fins, window screens), and a storm record for your area on or near the reported date. A roof can be old and still be storm-damaged. Age affects depreciation; it does not by itself decide coverage.</p>

<h3>2. Repair vs. full replacement</h3>
<p>Insurers sometimes approve a handful of shingles or a single slope when the damage is spread across the roof. Whether a repair is practical depends on the number and distribution of damaged shingles, whether the existing shingles can be lifted without breaking (brittle, older shingles often cannot), and whether matching material is available.</p>

<h3>3. Matching</h3>
<p>If one slope is replaced and the shingle line has been discontinued or has weathered to a different color, the new section may not match. Whether the insurer must pay to create a reasonably uniform appearance depends on your policy language and the facts, so ask the insurer to explain its position in writing and keep samples or product information if a roofer identifies a discontinued shingle.</p>

<h3>4. Code-required items</h3>
<p>The City of San Marcos enforces the 2021 International Codes, including the 2021 International Residential Code, under Ordinance 2023-09, in effect since April 1, 2023. The IRC includes requirements for asphalt shingle roofs such as underlayment and drip edge, and it limits recovering over existing roofs that already have multiple layers. One local amendment is worth knowing: <strong>a roof covering replacement permit is not required in San Marcos unless the replacement roof is heavier than the existing roof covering.</strong> No permit does not mean no code requirements. Whether your insurer pays for code-related costs depends on your policy and any ordinance or law coverage. Our <a href="/local-building-codes/">local building codes page</a> and <a href="/building-codes-insurance-repairs-san-marcos/">guide to building codes and insurance repairs</a> go deeper. Homeowners in Kyle, Buda, Wimberley, or New Braunfels should check with their own city’s permit office.</p>

<h3>5. How the policy values the roof</h3>
<p>Two homeowners with identical hail damage can receive very different checks because of how their policies value the roof. Read your declarations page and endorsements before you assume what you will be paid.</p>

<table>
<thead><tr><th>Roof valuation</th><th>How it generally works</th><th>What to watch for</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Replacement cost value (RCV)</td><td>The insurer usually pays actual cash value first, then releases recoverable depreciation after the work is completed.</td><td>Deadlines to finish repairs and submit invoices; keep the contractor’s final invoice.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Actual cash value (ACV)</td><td>Pays replacement cost minus depreciation for wear and age. You pay the difference.</td><td>How depreciation was calculated, and whether it was applied to labor as well as materials.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Roof payment schedule</td><td>Some Texas policies pay roof losses as a percentage that declines with the roof’s age.</td><td>The roof’s age the insurer used; confirm it against permits, invoices, or closing documents.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Percentage wind/hail deductible</td><td>Deductible is a percentage of the dwelling (Coverage A) limit rather than a flat dollar amount.</td><td>On a large home, the deductible may exceed a small repair estimate.</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>

<p>The Texas Department of Insurance gives a simple example in its home insurance guide: a replacement cost policy pays for a new roof at today’s prices, while actual cash value subtracts depreciation for the roof’s age, which can leave a large gap for the homeowner to cover. Our article on <a href="/replacement-cost-vs-actual-cash-value-texas/">replacement cost vs. actual cash value in Texas</a> walks through the math.</p>

