<h2>What a roof damage public adjuster actually does</h2>

<p>A public adjuster is licensed by the Texas Department of Insurance under Texas Insurance Code Chapter 4102 to represent policyholders in property claims. On a roof claim, the work usually looks like this:</p>

<ul>
<li><strong>Policy review.</strong> Reading the declarations, roof endorsements, deductibles, ordinance or law coverage, and loss-settlement terms before anyone argues about numbers.</li>
<li><strong>Roof and property inspection.</strong> Slope-by-slope photos with labels, test-square documentation, close-ups of impacts and creases, and collateral damage on gutters, vents, fences, siding, windows, and outbuildings. Interior stains and attic decking are checked for leaks.</li>
<li><strong>Storm verification.</strong> Matching the date of loss to NOAA storm reports and other weather data for your location.</li>
<li><strong>Line-item estimate.</strong> Building a detailed estimate that includes tear-off, underlayment, starter, drip edge, ridge, flashing, pipe jacks, vents, steep or high-roof charges, and code items, so the claim reflects what a proper repair requires.</li>
<li><strong>Presentation and follow-up.</strong> Submitting the documentation, meeting the insurer’s adjuster on re-inspection, responding to requests, and pursuing <a href="/services/insurance-claim-supplements/">supplements</a> when hidden damage turns up during tear-off.</li>
</ul>

<p>Under Texas law, a public adjuster cannot act as your roofer on a claim they are adjusting, and roofing contractors cannot act as your public adjuster. That separation keeps the roles clear: the adjuster documents and presents the claim, and you choose the contractor who does the work.</p>

<figure><img src="/assets/img/stock/hail-impact-shingle-closeup.webp" alt="Close-up of a hand pointing to hail impact marks on an asphalt shingle with a hailstone inset" width="1536" height="1024" loading="lazy" decoding="async"><figcaption>Fresh hail impacts often show exposed mat and crisp edges, which helps separate storm damage from age-related wear.</figcaption></figure>

<h2>Insurer’s adjuster vs. roof damage public adjuster</h2>

<table>
<thead><tr><th></th><th>Insurer’s adjuster (staff or independent)</th><th>Public adjuster</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Who they represent</td><td>The insurance company</td><td>You, the policyholder</td></tr>
<tr><td>Who pays them</td><td>The insurer or the firm the insurer hires</td><td>You, under a written contract (capped at 10% of the settlement in Texas)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Time on your roof</td><td>Often limited, especially during busy storm seasons</td><td>Set by the scope of the claim</td></tr>
<tr><td>Estimate</td><td>Prepared to the insurer’s guidelines</td><td>Prepared from your documentation and the policy terms</td></tr>
<tr><td>Licensing in Texas</td><td>Licensed adjuster</td><td>Licensed public adjuster under Insurance Code Chapter 4102</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>

<p>Many insurer adjusters are careful and fair. The point is not that they are adversaries; it is that their job is to evaluate the claim for the company. A public adjuster gives the policyholder a professional of their own. For more on the difference, see <a href="/public-adjuster-vs-insurance-adjuster/">public adjuster vs. insurance adjuster</a>.</p>

<h2>When a roof damage public adjuster may be worth calling</h2>

<p>Not every roof claim needs outside help. If the insurer inspected promptly, the estimate covers a full replacement your roofer agrees with, and the depreciation and deductible look right, you may be fine handling it yourself. A conversation with a public adjuster in San Marcos may be worthwhile when:</p>

<ul>
<li>The claim was denied as wear and tear, pre-existing damage, or “no storm-created damage.”</li>
<li>The insurer approved spot repairs or one slope, but damage appears on several slopes.</li>
<li>The estimate leaves out items your roofer says are needed, such as drip edge, underlayment, flashing, or code items.</li>
<li>Depreciation or a roof payment schedule took a large bite and you are not sure the roof age is right.</li>
<li>Interior damage, metal outbuildings, fences, or other structures were left off the claim.</li>
<li>The claim involves a commercial or low-slope roof, a large home, or a percentage deductible.</li>
<li>You simply do not have the time to manage inspections, re-inspections, and paperwork.</li>
</ul>

<p>If you already have an insurer estimate in hand, our guide on <a href="/how-to-review-underpaid-roof-insurance-estimate/">how to review an underpaid roof estimate</a> shows what to check line by line. If the claim was <a href="/services/underpaid-insurance-claims/">underpaid</a> or <a href="/services/denied-insurance-claims/">denied</a>, those service pages explain the options.</p>

<h2>How to protect your roof claim in the first two weeks</h2>

<ol>
<li><strong>Stay safe and stop further damage.</strong> Arrange a tarp or temporary repair if water is getting in, and keep the receipts. Most policies expect reasonable steps to prevent more damage.</li>
<li><strong>Photograph before anything is touched.</strong> Wide shots of each side of the house, close-ups of gutters, vents, AC units, screens, fences, and any interior stains. Date-stamped photos are better.</li>
<li><strong>Write down the storm date.</strong> Note when it happened and what you saw. Check it against NOAA records or our <a href="/storm-lookup/">storm lookup</a>.</li>
<li><strong>Report the claim promptly.</strong> Policies require prompt notice, and waiting can create avoidable questions about when the damage happened.</li>
<li><strong>Be present for the inspection.</strong> Ask the adjuster which slopes were inspected, how many damaged shingles were counted, and whether interior and outbuilding damage was reviewed.</li>
<li><strong>Get everything in writing.</strong> Ask for the estimate, the photos, and any engineering or weather report the insurer relied on.</li>
<li><strong>Do not sign a release you do not understand.</strong> Accepting a payment is not always final, but a signed release can be. Ask questions first.</li>
</ol>

<p>Our <a href="/claim-documentation-checklist/">claim documentation checklist</a> is a printable version of these steps.</p>

<h2>What happens if you and the insurer still disagree?</h2>

<p>If the insurer agrees the roof was damaged but you disagree about the amount, most Texas homeowners policies contain an <a href="/services/insurance-appraisal/">appraisal clause</a>. Either side can demand appraisal, each side picks an appraiser, the appraisers choose an umpire, and an award agreed to by any two is generally binding on the amount of loss. Appraisal does not decide coverage questions, such as whether damage is wear and tear.</p>

<p>For coverage disputes, possible bad-faith conduct, or questions about deadlines to sue, talk to a Texas attorney. Many Texas policies set a contractual deadline to file suit, and weather-related claim lawsuits have pre-suit notice requirements under Insurance Code Chapter 542A. A public adjuster cannot give legal advice.</p>

<h2>Choosing a roof damage public adjuster in San Marcos</h2>

<p>If you decide to get help, look for these basics:</p>

<ul>
<li><strong>An active Texas public adjuster license.</strong> You can confirm a license and ask about complaints through the TDI Help Line at 800-252-3439.</li>
<li><strong>A clear written contract.</strong> Texas requires TDI-approved contract terms, the statement “WE REPRESENT THE INSURED ONLY,” and a 72-hour right to cancel.</li>
<li><strong>Fee transparency.</strong> Fees are disclosed in a written contract before any work begins; Texas law caps public adjuster compensation at 10% of the insurance settlement. TDI notes you may still owe a fee even if the insurer does not increase its offer, so ask how the fee is calculated.</li>
<li><strong>Roof-specific experience</strong> with shingle, metal, and low-slope roofs and with the wear-and-tear and matching issues described above.</li>
<li><strong>No pressure.</strong> Texas public adjusters may not solicit door to door during a natural disaster or after 9 p.m. Be wary of anyone who promises a new roof or a specific payout.</li>
</ul>

<p>If you are comparing San Marcos public adjusters, ask each one the same questions and compare the answers. San Marcos Public Adjuster is a public adjusting service of [[company]], Texas Department of Insurance license #[[license]], serving San Marcos, Hays County, and surrounding Central Texas communities including <a href="/service-areas/kyle/">Kyle</a>, Buda, Wimberley, and New Braunfels. Our <a href="/services/roof-damage-insurance-claims/">roof damage insurance claims</a> and <a href="/services/hail-damage-claims/">hail damage claims</a> pages describe how we handle these files.</p>

<h2>Bottom line</h2>

<p>A roof claim looks simple from the driveway and gets complicated on the roof: damage that hides, disputes over age, matching, code items, and policy terms that change the check. Thorough documentation and a careful reading of the policy are what move those issues. If you want a second set of eyes on your roof claim, a <a href="/">San Marcos public adjuster</a> can review your estimate, photos, and policy and tell you honestly whether outside help makes sense. Start with a <a href="/free-claim-review/">Free Claim Review</a>, or call [[phone]].</p>
